---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Architecture — Nalinka Heshan"
	description="How nalinkaheshan.dev deploys itself: static Astro on S3 and CloudFront via Terraform, Cloudflare DNS, and GitHub Actions assuming an IAM role over OIDC with no stored AWS keys."
>
	<h1>How this site deploys itself</h1>

	<section>
		<p>
			This site is a static Astro build — plain HTML and CSS, no client-side
			framework. It sits in a private S3 bucket, not using S3's static website
			hosting feature. CloudFront reads it through Origin Access Control, and the
			bucket policy only allows reads from this one distribution's ARN, not from
			any CloudFront distribution in the account.
		</p>
		<p>
			Pushing to <code>master</code> triggers a GitHub Actions workflow: it builds
			the site, assumes an IAM role over OIDC, syncs the build to the bucket, and
			invalidates the CloudFront distribution. There's no manual step in between.
		</p>
		<p>
			There are no AWS access keys stored in the repository or in GitHub secrets.
			GitHub Actions authenticates by requesting a short-lived OIDC token from
			GitHub's own token endpoint and exchanging it for temporary credentials
			scoped to one IAM role. The role's trust policy only accepts that token for
			this exact repository and branch, and its permissions cover writing to one
			S3 bucket and invalidating one CloudFront distribution — nothing else.
		</p>
		<p>
			The infrastructure — the S3 bucket, the CloudFront distribution, the ACM
			certificate, and the IAM role itself — is defined in Terraform, not clicked
			together by hand in the AWS console. State is local, not remote.
		</p>
		<p>
			DNS is Cloudflare, not Route 53. The domain is registered through Cloudflare
			Registrar, and its registration agreement requires Cloudflare's own
			nameservers at every plan tier — there's no delegating to Route 53. The ACM
			validation record and the apex record pointing at CloudFront are both added
			by hand in Cloudflare, with the proxy switched off. With the proxy on,
			Cloudflare would terminate TLS with its own certificate in front of
			CloudFront, which would make the ACM certificate pointless.
		</p>
		<p>
			GitHub changed how it writes identity into the OIDC token: for repositories
			created after 15 July 2026, the subject claim embeds the numeric owner and
			repository IDs, not just their names —
			<code>repo:nalinka1@35029715/nalinka-heshan@1358027744:ref:refs/heads/master</code>,
			not the plain <code>repo:nalinka1/nalinka-heshan:ref:refs/heads/master</code>
			that most OIDC tutorials still assume. I found this out by decoding the
			token from a live workflow run after role assumption kept failing against a
			trust policy that looked correct.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="links-heading">
		<h2 id="links-heading">Links</h2>
		<ul class="rail-links">
			<li><a href="https://github.com/nalinka1/nalinka-heshan/tree/master/terraform">Terraform directory</a></li>
			<li><a href="https://github.com/nalinka1/nalinka-heshan/blob/master/.github/workflows/deploy.yml">Deploy workflow</a></li>
		</ul>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	h1 {
		margin-bottom: var(--space-8);
	}

	h2 {
		margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
	}

	section + section {
		margin-top: var(--space-8);
		padding-top: var(--space-8);
		border-top: var(--rule-width) solid var(--rule);
	}

	.rail-links {
		list-style: none;
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0;
	}

	.rail-links li {
		margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
	}

	.rail-links li:last-child {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}
</style>
